I’ll begin with a physical inventory of the metadata, pattern, reusable scripts, and candidate structure library. I’ll keep that screening fit provisional, compare plausible polymorphs under the same nuisance model, then rebuild the accepted inventory in a clean GSAS-II project and verify all exports against it.

The chemistry points first to Sr–W–O products plus possible unreacted WO₃/SrCO₃, but I’m not treating that as the phase list. I’m now extracting diagnostic peak families and enumerating exact-composition records (including polymorph/source metadata) so candidates can be screened on symmetry, occupancy, and geometry before any definitive refinement.

The strongest family is consistent with scheelite-type SrWO₄, but it is displaced to lower 2θ and several strong lines belong to another family near 23–26°. That makes a single angular correction and residual WO₃ plausible, not yet proven. I’m screening scheelite parents, every available WO₃ polymorph, both SrCO₃ records, and the only exact higher strontium tungstate entry; a zero scale will not be my sole rejection criterion.

The inventory now resolves three independent, chemically balanced families under a common ≈−0.61° zero correction: scheelite SrWO₄, room-temperature WO₃, and orthorhombic SrCO₃. For example, the corrected WO₃ triplet maps to 22.51/22.94/23.83°, while the SrCO₃ doublet maps to 24.65/25.25°. I’m moving from peak matching to equal-schedule whole-pattern tests, including adverse polymorphs and alternate structure parents, before accepting that inventory.

The equal-schedule screening is still running across 17 fresh projects. Each branch refits background, all phase scales, zero, cells, and the same common U/W profile, then reopens and replays the full range; this is deliberately slower than stick-pattern matching because it exposes false polymorphs through unique-peak residuals and nonphysical scales.

Early whole-pattern results confirm the three-phase inventory at positive scales. Among scheelite parents, the exact-W CaWO₄ prototype currently fits best and has sensible adapted W–O/Sr–O geometry; the pressure-stabilized fergusonite replacement collapses to the nonnegative scale limit and destabilizes its cell, so that branch is rejected. Room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ records are far better than the first alternate polymorph tested. The provisional U/W fit tried to drive U negative, so that profile branch is invalid and will be restarted with corrected nonnegative bounds as required.

I’ve restarted the key comparisons with the corrected GSAS-II variable bounds and a nonnegative W/X common profile (U held neutral at zero). This repeat is also isolating the two carbonate structures and the high-pressure Sr₃W₂O₉ hypothesis in fresh projects, avoiding numerical state carried over from malformed rejected records.

The corrected replay keeps the same ranking: CaWO₄→SrWO₄ is the best scheelite parent (Rwp 6.94% versus 7.11% for EuWO₄→SrWO₄), and neutron-derived monoclinic WO₃ record 2311041 is best (6.94% versus 7.31% for the alternate room-temperature record). The common profile is now valid and interior: W≈43.8 and X≈1.77, with no frozen/bound variables.

The inventory is now stable: orthorhombic SrCO₃ is retained, while the high-temperature carbonate and pressure-synthesized Sr₃W₂O₉ both drive to the nonnegative scale limit and then lose meaningful cell refinement. I’m starting the mandated clean final pass from only SrWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + Pnma SrCO₃, and will use fresh branch projects for asymmetry, per-phase size/strain, and texture tests so rejected nuisance terms cannot contaminate the submitted project.

The clean project is refining the entire measured range (9.903–100.095°) with dual Cu Kα wavelengths, Poisson working weights, ten background terms, three positive phase scales, refined cells, and only zero as the angular correction. The profile branches are being saved and reopened between changes; U and SH/L are being challenged in separate copies so any bound hit cannot leak into the accepted W/X project.

The per-phase broadening challenge is underway. Each of the six branches starts from the same reopened W/X project and adds exactly one isotropic term—size-only or strain-only—for one phase, with positive physical bounds; common profile, cells, scales, zero, and background are reoptimized and replayed. I’ll retain a term only if it stays interior, improves the profile, and does not merely trade against W/X.

The broadening tests are decisive: SrWO₄ size is singular and its strain term hits zero; both are rejected. SrCO₃ size/strain changes are statistically negligible and are rejected. WO₃ alone supports phase-specific broadening—size gives the better single-term fit (≈0.123 µm, Rwp 6.706%); strain is also positive but weaker, so I’m now testing their combination for aliasing before choosing one. I’m also standardizing the neutron WO₃ cell to remove the avoidable metric correlation caused by its unconventional β≈136° setting.

The first conventional-cell replay exposed an importer-setting mismatch, not a physical difference: GSAS-II was silently using P2₁/c operations on P2₁/n coordinates. I removed the conflicting International Tables tag and verified the phase now imports explicitly as P2₁/n with the neutron structure unchanged. The clean replay is being rerun; this should retain the fit while removing the prior cell-metric correlation.

The standardized P2₁/n replay now reproduces the original fit exactly (Rwp 6.706%) and removes the cell-metric correlation. I’m testing only physically coherent intensity changes at this stage: the three independent SrWO₄ oxygen coordinates (the cross-chemistry prototype’s only free internal site) and one nonnegative extinction coefficient per phase in separate projects.

The oxygen-coordinate branch lowers Rwp but fails the physical screen: it stretches tetrahedral W–O to 2.04 Å and compresses Sr–O to 2.40–2.46 Å, so it is rejected as an intensity surrogate. SrWO₄ and WO₃ extinction hit zero; SrCO₃ extinction is 98% correlated with its scale and gives no meaningful improvement. I’m now comparing no texture against symmetry-distinct March–Dollase axes for each phase.

Nine texture branches are being replayed: [001], [100], and [110] for tetragonal SrWO₄; the three crystallographic axes for monoclinic WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃. Each is a single bounded parameter tested against the same no-texture project, so an apparent gain must be coherent across several reflection families rather than compensate for a changed profile or phase fraction.

Texture is clearly supported for two phases and axis-discriminating: SrWO₄ [110] gives Rwp 5.705% (other axes 6.22–6.43%), and WO₃ [001] gives 5.661% (other axes 6.07–6.64%). Carbonate axes improve only to 6.666–6.704% and are less distinct. I’m combining the two strong, interior terms first, checking their correlation, then retesting carbonate texture against that combined model.

The physical inventory is stable at three phases: scheelite SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and strontianite SrCO₃. I’ve rejected alternate polymorphs that either lost their scale or produced unstable cells; I’m now finishing the clean profile pass by combining only the two strong, phase-specific texture corrections and testing whether the much weaker carbonate effect survives.

The two major texture terms remain interior and independent when combined (SrWO₄ [110], WO₃ [001]); they reduce \(R_{wp}\) from 6.71% to 4.42% without introducing new high correlations. SrCO₃ [001] gives a smaller but repeatable improvement to 4.36% and beats both other symmetry-distinct axes; I’m checking that this comes from several carbonate reflection families rather than one overlapping peak before retaining it.

The carbonate correction is supported across independent low-, mid-, and high-angle families—not just one peak: the 111, 102, 013/202, and 104 regions each show large local \(\chi^2\) reductions, with a total \(\Delta\chi^2\) of about 516 for one interior parameter. I’m retaining SrCO₃ [001] texture and moving to the mandatory save–reopen full-range replay, export, geometry audit, and project/profile consistency checks.

The HAP audit caught an important hidden issue before export: GSAS-II’s nominal fixed defaults—1 µm size and 1000 µstrain—are finite broadening contributions, not neutral values. That means the otherwise clean pass still embedded phase broadening in every phase. I’m correcting those to effectively infinite size and zero strain and restarting the entire final profile/size/texture schedule, as the requested protocol requires.

With truly neutral HAP terms, the conclusions are clearer: SrWO₄ size is singular and strain hits zero; WO₃ has reproducible broadening (size-only is the stronger branch); SrCO₃ size is only marginal but interior. I’m now testing WO₃ size+strain and then re-testing carbonate size after WO₃ size is present, so the final decision is based on the converged common profile rather than a hidden default.

The carbonate size term survives after WO₃ broadening (0.311(74) µm, no new high correlation), so I’m retaining it. Its added-strain branch tries to go negative (−4024 µstrain) and correlates strongly with size, so that branch is rejected as a sharpening surrogate. The accepted broadening model is therefore size-only for WO₃ and SrCO₃, with SrWO₄ left neutral.

The post-texture pruning check changed one decision: SrCO₃ size drifted to 1.58(1.23) µm and is no longer supported once preferred orientation is modeled. I’m removing it and replaying the intensity/texture schedule from the WO₃-size-only project. GSAS-II also caps its “effectively infinite” fixed size at 10 µm, so I’m recording 10 µm as the neutral fixed value explicitly.

The pruned model is stable: only WO₃ retains size broadening (0.1479(41) µm); both other phase sizes are fixed at the neutral 10 µm and all strains are zero/fixed. The three texture parameters remain highly significant and interior, with no added correlation above 0.8. I’m now creating the exact final project and exports from a fresh two-pass save–reopen replay.

The final replay converged cleanly over all 8,301 points: \(R_{wp}=4.1392\%\), \(R_p=3.0829\%\), GOF 1.4492, no frozen variables, and only the expected W/X correlation (−0.870). The crystalline fractions are 62.186% SrWO₄, 22.873% WO₃, and 14.942% SrCO₃. I’m performing the last independent CIF geometry/composition check and residual-family audit before writing the report.

The independent checks pass: all exported sites are fully occupied, bond geometries are chemically plausible, `fit.csv` has the full 8,301-point profile with the correct difference sign, fractions sum exactly to 1, and reopening `final.gpx` reproduces the submitted profile, statistics, and fractions. No unassigned peak family warrants a fourth phase.

Resolved three crystalline phases:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| SrWO₄, I 4₁/a | 62.19% |
| WO₃, P 2₁/n | 22.87% |
| SrCO₃, Pnma | 14.94% |

Final fit: \(R_{wp}=4.139\%\), \(R_p=3.083\%\), GOF = 1.449. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; practical uncertainty is approximately 1–3 absolute wt%.

